1
00:01:15,609 --> 00:01:17,271
Hi.

2
00:01:18,278 --> 00:01:20,838
I'm Leanne Mclntyre.
I'm an attorney.

3
00:01:21,014 --> 00:01:22,778
My client asked
me to give you this.

4
00:01:22,950 --> 00:01:26,546
He caught someone trying to plant
it in his house yesterday afternoon.

5
00:01:26,720 --> 00:01:30,555
He's fairly certain it's the same
gun used to kill Dennis Spiers.

6
00:01:30,724 --> 00:01:33,284
And the intruder, whom
he didn't recognize,

7
00:01:33,460 --> 00:01:35,861
is trying to frame
him for that murder.

8
00:01:36,029 --> 00:01:37,463
Have a nice day. Bye-bye.

9
00:01:37,631 --> 00:01:39,361
Hold it. Hold it.

10
00:01:39,533 --> 00:01:41,263
Your client got a name?

11
00:01:41,435 --> 00:01:42,869
Well, of course. Bye.

12
00:01:43,036 --> 00:01:46,165
It wouldn't be Ed Wingate,
by any chance, would it?

13
00:01:47,007 --> 00:01:49,806
- Oh, is Ed Wingate a suspect?
- We searched his house

14
00:01:49,977 --> 00:01:52,003
and found Dennis Spiers'
watch and gold cufflinks.

15
00:01:52,179 --> 00:01:53,374
So you tell Ed Wingate

16
00:01:53,547 --> 00:01:56,745
the sooner he gets his butt in here
to answer some questions, the better.

17
00:01:56,917 --> 00:02:00,820
If I see him, I'm gonna
tell him. Bye-bye.

18
00:02:14,267 --> 00:02:17,294
You're making me very nervous.

19
00:02:17,471 --> 00:02:21,636
I'm not making a pass at you,
Bink. I just want a little information.

20
00:02:21,808 --> 00:02:24,676
I shouldn't be talking to you.

21
00:02:25,145 --> 00:02:27,910
I'm not a murderer, Bink.

22
00:02:29,516 --> 00:02:31,280
Keep eating.

23
00:02:31,451 --> 00:02:34,285
Make it look like you're
chewing when you talk.

24
00:02:34,955 --> 00:02:37,720
Did you tell anyone about
the fight Spiers and I had?

25
00:02:37,891 --> 00:02:42,625
Alan Pierce, Brenda Farrell,
some police lieutenant.

26
00:02:44,064 --> 00:02:46,761
They were asking me a whole
bunch of questions about Dennis

27
00:02:46,933 --> 00:02:48,401
the morning after he was killed.

28
00:02:48,935 --> 00:02:51,928
Well, I'm sorry.
It just slipped out.

29
00:02:52,105 --> 00:02:54,097
That's okay.

30
00:02:55,142 --> 00:02:57,577
Why were they asking you
questions about Dennis?

31
00:02:57,744 --> 00:02:59,542
They knew he called
me the night before.

32
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:01,204
About what?

33
00:03:01,381 --> 00:03:03,221
He was pretty sure
he had found the house

34
00:03:03,383 --> 00:03:05,318
where the Sanborn
kid was being held.

35
00:03:05,485 --> 00:03:08,284
Wanted me to analyze some motor
oil he'd found in the driveway there.

36
00:03:08,755 --> 00:03:10,519
Motor oil?

37
00:03:11,258 --> 00:03:12,317
Where is this house?

38
00:03:12,926 --> 00:03:13,950
He didn't say.

39
00:03:14,127 --> 00:03:16,892
- Do you still have the oil?
- Yeah.

40
00:03:18,765 --> 00:03:22,793
I'm going to send you
another sample this afternoon.

41
00:03:22,969 --> 00:03:28,966
I want you to compare the two, see
if they came from the same engine.

42
00:03:29,142 --> 00:03:32,078
Ugh. If they find
out what I'm doing...

43
00:03:32,245 --> 00:03:34,043
They won't.

44
00:03:37,117 --> 00:03:38,176
I'll be in touch.

45
00:03:41,688 --> 00:03:43,247
Hey.

46
00:03:46,059 --> 00:03:48,085
Your slip is showing.

47
00:03:50,097 --> 00:03:51,622
Thank you.

48
00:04:01,108 --> 00:04:02,906
I can't tell you
how worried I was.

49
00:04:03,076 --> 00:04:04,442
I felt helpless.

50
00:04:04,611 --> 00:04:07,103
Pop, it was the worst
four days of my life.

51
00:04:07,280 --> 00:04:09,715
Worse than the time Brewster
Sherwood took your bike apart

52
00:04:09,883 --> 00:04:11,977
and hung the pieces
down in the outhouse?

53
00:04:12,152 --> 00:04:14,087
- Oh, much worse, ten times worse.
- Ha, ha.

54
00:04:14,254 --> 00:04:15,415
Although that was pretty bad.

55
00:04:15,589 --> 00:04:17,524
- Yeah. Pass the ketchup.
- Uh...

56
00:04:17,691 --> 00:04:19,785
Anything else you want?
Do you want another hot dog?

57
00:04:19,960 --> 00:04:22,054
I already had two. What
are you trying to do to me?

58
00:04:22,863 --> 00:04:25,103
You look a little thin. I'm
just trying to fatten you up.

59
00:04:26,466 --> 00:04:28,059
There is one thing
you can do for me.

60
00:04:28,235 --> 00:04:30,466
Anything. You just name
it. Anything you want.

61
00:04:30,637 --> 00:04:32,333
Be Barry and Tracey's lawyer.

62
00:04:36,109 --> 00:04:39,170
You want me to defend the
people who kidnapped you?

63
00:04:39,746 --> 00:04:40,805
They're broke.

64
00:04:40,981 --> 00:04:43,473
Unless you do it, they're liable
to get some public defender

65
00:04:43,650 --> 00:04:45,650
who doesn't know diddly
and doesn't give two hoots.

66
00:04:45,819 --> 00:04:47,811
You care what happens to
people who kidnapped you?

67
00:04:47,988 --> 00:04:49,354
They're nice kids.

68
00:04:51,291 --> 00:04:52,554
Stockholm Syndrome.

69
00:04:53,927 --> 00:04:55,452
The what?

70
00:04:55,629 --> 00:04:57,655
Stockholm Syndrome.

71
00:04:57,831 --> 00:05:00,960
People who're held hostage often
wind up sympathizing with their captors,

72
00:05:01,134 --> 00:05:03,603
sometimes to the point
of defecting to their cause.

73
00:05:03,770 --> 00:05:05,330
I've read about it.
I've seen it on TV.

74
00:05:05,472 --> 00:05:07,498
I never thought it'd
happen to my father.

75
00:05:07,674 --> 00:05:08,767
Who's defecting?

76
00:05:08,942 --> 00:05:11,434
I just think they deserve
a fair shake, that's all.

77
00:05:12,846 --> 00:05:14,371
Billy.

78
00:05:15,081 --> 00:05:17,050
Where you been? I been
looking all over for you.

79
00:05:17,217 --> 00:05:20,187
How come you won't
answer my calls?

80
00:05:21,021 --> 00:05:22,683
You know this person?

81
00:05:22,856 --> 00:05:24,916
Pop, it's Ben.

82
00:05:25,292 --> 00:05:28,126
I knew a man named Ben once.

83
00:05:28,295 --> 00:05:31,197
- Thought he was my friend.
- Pop.

84
00:05:31,464 --> 00:05:34,866
Then one day I
saw his true colors.

85
00:05:35,735 --> 00:05:40,435
My life was at stake,
and what did he do?

86
00:05:40,874 --> 00:05:42,866
He bickered over
what it was worth.

87
00:05:43,043 --> 00:05:46,036
I wasn't bickering.
I was stalling.

88
00:05:46,213 --> 00:05:52,312
Tell Mr. "Let's Make A Deal" that as
far as I'm concerned he no longer exists.

89
00:05:52,485 --> 00:05:53,509
Yeah?

90
00:05:53,687 --> 00:05:57,419
Well, you tell Mr. "Let's Be
Ungrateful As Hell" that he's a jerk.

91
00:06:39,032 --> 00:06:40,261
How'd you get in here?

92
00:06:40,700 --> 00:06:42,931
Quietly. I didn't
mean to scare you.

93
00:06:43,103 --> 00:06:44,264
Ugh.

94
00:06:44,437 --> 00:06:46,065
I wonder if you'd
do me a small favor.

95
00:06:46,239 --> 00:06:47,263
Oh.

96
00:06:48,942 --> 00:06:51,935
Would you deliver this
oil sample to the FBI lab,

97
00:06:52,112 --> 00:06:54,843
see that it gets to a
techie named Bink.

98
00:06:55,415 --> 00:06:56,883
Thanks.

99
00:06:58,718 --> 00:07:01,347
- Can I ask you something?
- Anything.

100
00:07:01,521 --> 00:07:04,491
Well, one day you come
in here in a Hawaiian shirt.

101
00:07:04,658 --> 00:07:07,526
The next day you come in
dressed like a construction worker.

102
00:07:07,694 --> 00:07:09,287
Sometimes I wear a dress.

103
00:07:10,397 --> 00:07:12,593
I wouldn't be surprised.

104
00:07:12,766 --> 00:07:16,259
You wear those
dark glasses at night.

105
00:07:16,436 --> 00:07:17,870
You're the wind.

106
00:07:18,038 --> 00:07:21,668
I really don't get that one.
How'd you get so far up in the FBI?

107
00:07:21,841 --> 00:07:23,469
Results.

108
00:07:23,643 --> 00:07:27,239
I work hard, go the extra
mile, get the job done.

109
00:07:27,714 --> 00:07:29,274
I'm really not very
different than you.

110
00:07:31,851 --> 00:07:33,820
- We're alike?
- Yeah.

111
00:07:33,987 --> 00:07:36,013
Ha, ha, I don't think so.

112
00:07:36,189 --> 00:07:42,891
- Do you dress like other lawyers?
- Um, I don't know.

113
00:07:43,063 --> 00:07:44,725
- Do you live in a big house?
- No.

114
00:07:44,898 --> 00:07:48,266
You figure, if you get
your client off, that's the job.

115
00:07:48,435 --> 00:07:51,701
Doesn't matter how you
look, or where you live.

116
00:07:52,138 --> 00:07:55,006
You won't even tell people
what law school you went to.

117
00:07:59,312 --> 00:08:02,976
You're all right,
Ed. You're all right.

118
00:08:03,917 --> 00:08:07,149
I wouldn't want you to marry
my daughter, but you're all right.

119
00:08:07,320 --> 00:08:08,754
Oh, that's okay.

120
00:08:08,922 --> 00:08:10,891
We're probably not
very compatible, anyway.

121
00:08:11,491 --> 00:08:14,222
- Huh?
- She takes herself too seriously.

122
00:08:48,528 --> 00:08:50,053
Yes?

123
00:08:52,665 --> 00:08:56,295
Well, Mr. Bingham, what brings
you up from the bowels of the lab?

124
00:08:57,170 --> 00:09:00,197
Well, something kind
of bizarre is going on,

125
00:09:00,373 --> 00:09:04,310
and I figured I'd better talk to
somebody before it got any bizarrer.

126
00:09:04,477 --> 00:09:06,002
Sit down.

127
00:09:07,247 --> 00:09:08,687
You know how Ed
Wingate always claims

128
00:09:08,848 --> 00:09:11,340
that some person planted
the gun in his house,

129
00:09:11,518 --> 00:09:13,077
the one that killed
Dennis Spiers,

130
00:09:13,253 --> 00:09:15,313
so it would look like he
was the one who offed him?

131
00:09:16,823 --> 00:09:17,847
Yes.

132
00:09:18,024 --> 00:09:20,721
- Well, I kind of ran into him today.
- You saw Ed Wingate?

133
00:09:20,894 --> 00:09:22,726
- Yes. Where?
- Well, actually...

134
00:09:22,896 --> 00:09:26,424
In a restaurant downtown, he
was doing one of his drag numbers.

135
00:09:26,599 --> 00:09:28,830
Anyway, he asked
me to analyze some oil

136
00:09:29,002 --> 00:09:32,302
that leaked from the car of the guy
that he says planted the gun in his house.

137
00:09:33,573 --> 00:09:35,872
I did. And here's
what's really weird.

138
00:09:36,042 --> 00:09:37,806
Not only talked to him,
you did him a favor?

139
00:09:37,977 --> 00:09:39,411
The man is wanted
for questioning.

140
00:09:39,579 --> 00:09:41,138
The oil that Ed
Wingate sent over

141
00:09:41,314 --> 00:09:44,443
matched the sample that Dennis
Spears sent over the night he died.

142
00:09:44,617 --> 00:09:46,415
Are you sure?

143
00:09:46,653 --> 00:09:49,418
Both samples came
from the same engine.

144
00:09:49,756 --> 00:09:50,780
I'm positive.

145
00:09:56,596 --> 00:10:00,931
Brenda? Oh, good, you're
still here. Come to my office.

146
00:10:01,234 --> 00:10:04,136
We may have a break
in the Sanborn case.

147
00:10:07,674 --> 00:10:08,869
Did you tell Ed Wingate?

148
00:10:09,042 --> 00:10:12,035
No. He, uh... He said he'd call.

149
00:10:12,879 --> 00:10:13,903
Good.

150
00:10:19,652 --> 00:10:21,245
Yo, Bink here.

151
00:10:21,421 --> 00:10:22,781
WINGATE: <i>It's Wingate.</i>

152
00:10:22,922 --> 00:10:25,187
<i>Have you compared
the oil samples yet?</i>

153
00:10:25,358 --> 00:10:27,691
Yep. I sure did.

154
00:10:28,628 --> 00:10:29,652
<i>And?</i>

155
00:10:31,931 --> 00:10:33,399
<i>And?</i>

156
00:10:33,666 --> 00:10:38,263
I can't hear you very well, uh, with
all that music there. Where are you?

157
00:10:38,438 --> 00:10:41,033
Never mind. Did they match?

158
00:10:41,207 --> 00:10:44,302
Well, uh, the oils
matched, yeah.

159
00:10:44,477 --> 00:10:48,938
But, uh, did they come from the
same engine? That's the tough part.

160
00:10:49,115 --> 00:10:50,981
Well, did they?

161
00:10:52,152 --> 00:10:55,611
Well, you see,

162
00:10:56,689 --> 00:11:00,319
first I filtered out all of
the particles and, uh...

163
00:11:00,493 --> 00:11:03,952
Did they or didn't they, Bink?

164
00:11:04,664 --> 00:11:06,189
<i>Yes.</i>

165
00:11:07,233 --> 00:11:09,293
How can you be sure?

166
00:11:10,336 --> 00:11:12,737
<i>Well, that's what I
been trying to tell you.</i>

167
00:11:12,906 --> 00:11:17,970
I mean, first I filtered all the
particles out from the sample.

168
00:11:18,478 --> 00:11:20,606
That wasn't easy, you know.

169
00:11:21,114 --> 00:11:22,707
<i>Oh, of course not.</i>

170
00:11:22,882 --> 00:11:24,908
<i>- Then what did you do?</i>
- Oh...

171
00:11:25,084 --> 00:11:26,677
Well, uh...

172
00:11:54,280 --> 00:11:55,771
Hi.

173
00:11:55,949 --> 00:11:57,383
You're with the FBI.

174
00:11:59,519 --> 00:12:01,750
Alan Pierce, special
agent in charge.

175
00:12:01,921 --> 00:12:03,981
This is Agent Farrell.

176
00:12:04,958 --> 00:12:07,257
Well, Agent
Wingate is long gone.

177
00:12:07,427 --> 00:12:10,761
He figured that you
traced the call, heh.

178
00:12:10,930 --> 00:12:13,024
- I'm Ben Matlock.
- No kidding?

179
00:12:13,199 --> 00:12:14,223
Yeah.

180
00:12:14,400 --> 00:12:15,891
Sit down. Have some pizza.

181
00:12:16,069 --> 00:12:18,095
Might as well. Ed
bought it, ha, ha.

182
00:12:18,271 --> 00:12:20,103
Vegetarian,
wouldn't you know it?

183
00:12:24,143 --> 00:12:25,167
Where is he?

184
00:12:25,345 --> 00:12:29,806
Oh, I don't know, and if I did,
I'd be honor bound not to tell you.

185
00:12:29,983 --> 00:12:31,007
Want my pineapple?

186
00:12:32,418 --> 00:12:34,182
I understand he
has some information

187
00:12:34,354 --> 00:12:37,324
that might help us solve
the Sanborn kidnapping case.

188
00:12:37,490 --> 00:12:39,425
Well, all Ed knows

189
00:12:39,592 --> 00:12:41,788
is that whoever
kidnapped Matthew

190
00:12:41,961 --> 00:12:44,931
is the same man that
he saw at his house

191
00:12:45,098 --> 00:12:48,728
planting the jewelry and the gun
that was used to kill Dennis Spiers.

192
00:12:48,901 --> 00:12:51,063
At least, the guy was
driving the same car.

193
00:12:52,038 --> 00:12:54,633
Well, Ed has got to come in
and tell us what he looked like.

194
00:12:54,807 --> 00:12:56,742
Well, he'd love to.

195
00:12:56,909 --> 00:13:00,243
But he's a little concerned
about something. What is that?

196
00:13:00,613 --> 00:13:02,445
Artichoke. MATLOCK: Ew.

197
00:13:02,949 --> 00:13:04,611
- You want it?
- Mr. Matlock.

198
00:13:06,119 --> 00:13:09,487
Uh, well, ahem,
the way Ed sees it,

199
00:13:09,822 --> 00:13:14,817
uh, the kidnapper somehow found
out that Dennis Spiers was onto him.

200
00:13:14,994 --> 00:13:20,297
So, he killed him, and then he
framed Ed to cover for himself.

201
00:13:20,466 --> 00:13:25,336
Uh, the problem is how did
Mr. Kidnapper know to frame Ed?

202
00:13:25,505 --> 00:13:29,340
I mean, he didn't know who
he was, or where he lived,

203
00:13:29,509 --> 00:13:33,037
or the fact that he had just had
a big fight with Dennis Spiers.

204
00:13:33,212 --> 00:13:35,511
- Unless, uh...
- Unless what?

205
00:13:38,151 --> 00:13:40,848
Somebody at the FBI told him.

206
00:13:41,220 --> 00:13:44,748
You suggesting that someone with
the FBI is in business with a kidnapper?

207
00:13:44,924 --> 00:13:48,258
Saints preserve us,
that's how it looks.

208
00:13:48,428 --> 00:13:50,021
You really believe that?

209
00:13:50,463 --> 00:13:51,931
Oh, saints preserve us.

210
00:13:52,098 --> 00:13:54,590
Then you are an idiot.

211
00:15:10,676 --> 00:15:13,612
Just relax. Relax. It's just me.

212
00:15:13,780 --> 00:15:17,842
Sorry I had to sneak in, but
they may be watching the house.

213
00:15:18,017 --> 00:15:19,576
Why are you dressed that way?

214
00:15:20,019 --> 00:15:22,079
Never mind. I
don't want to know.

215
00:15:22,255 --> 00:15:24,156
No, no, no, Ben. Ben.

216
00:15:24,323 --> 00:15:25,689
We have to talk.

217
00:15:25,858 --> 00:15:27,087
Call me in the morning.

218
00:15:27,260 --> 00:15:29,957
They may have tapped the
phone. Oh, come on. Ben.

219
00:15:30,129 --> 00:15:31,649
You weren't sleeping
that well, anyway.

220
00:15:31,798 --> 00:15:33,096
Oh, it was the pizza.

221
00:15:33,266 --> 00:15:37,670
Whoever heard of
putting raisins on a pizza.

222
00:15:37,837 --> 00:15:39,328
There were no
raisins on that pizza.

223
00:15:44,310 --> 00:15:47,576
Listen, uh, I followed Alan
Pierce and Brenda Farrell

224
00:15:47,747 --> 00:15:49,579
after they left the
pizzeria last night.

225
00:15:49,749 --> 00:15:51,342
You did?

226
00:15:51,784 --> 00:15:54,549
I figured they'd be pretty
shaken up after they talked to you.

227
00:15:54,720 --> 00:15:57,690
Oh, uh, they might
have arrested you.

228
00:15:57,857 --> 00:15:59,553
Not me.

229
00:15:59,725 --> 00:16:02,661
Oh, that's right.
You're the wind.

230
00:16:05,598 --> 00:16:06,793
Where'd they go?

231
00:16:07,667 --> 00:16:11,866
Alan Pierce took Brenda Farrell
back to the office to get her car,

232
00:16:12,038 --> 00:16:14,132
where they split up.

233
00:16:14,307 --> 00:16:19,007
Instinct told me to
follow her, which I did,

234
00:16:19,178 --> 00:16:21,579
all the way to South Carolina,

235
00:16:21,747 --> 00:16:25,047
where she has a very nice
beach house right on the water,

236
00:16:25,218 --> 00:16:28,985
with a very nice Porsche
tucked in the garage.

237
00:16:29,755 --> 00:16:31,485
I'll bet she spends
her weekends there.

238
00:16:33,259 --> 00:16:34,955
Hmm, mm-hm.

239
00:16:36,195 --> 00:16:37,629
Is she married?

240
00:16:38,164 --> 00:16:39,792
- Divorced.
- Oh.

241
00:16:41,901 --> 00:16:44,132
- Alimony?
- Zip.

242
00:16:44,303 --> 00:16:45,566
Oh.

243
00:16:45,738 --> 00:16:47,297
Ben,

244
00:16:48,508 --> 00:16:49,874
I have a plan.

245
00:16:52,178 --> 00:16:53,669
Let's talk about
it in the morning.

246
00:16:55,581 --> 00:16:57,209
Ben.

247
00:16:59,685 --> 00:17:00,812
It is morning.

248
00:17:14,300 --> 00:17:15,962
Miss Harrington.

249
00:17:17,236 --> 00:17:18,670
Please.

250
00:17:19,872 --> 00:17:22,671
So, Mr. Lewis, how are you?

251
00:17:22,842 --> 00:17:24,538
I'm fine.

252
00:17:25,011 --> 00:17:26,411
First off,

253
00:17:26,579 --> 00:17:28,775
I want to assure you that
we have a very strong case

254
00:17:28,948 --> 00:17:30,780
against Mr. Feldman
and Miss Riggins,

255
00:17:30,950 --> 00:17:34,148
and I intend to prosecute
them to the full extent of the law.

256
00:17:34,320 --> 00:17:36,653
- I kind of wish you wouldn't.
- I beg your pardon?

257
00:17:36,822 --> 00:17:38,313
I'd like to see them get off.

258
00:17:38,824 --> 00:17:40,656
Stockholm Syndrome.

259
00:17:44,397 --> 00:17:45,524
Well, is he getting help?

260
00:17:46,098 --> 00:17:48,158
Oh, no, no, he doesn't
think he needs it.

261
00:17:48,334 --> 00:17:50,803
You know, you're talking
about me and I'm right here.

262
00:17:51,771 --> 00:17:56,266
Mr. Lewis, kidnapping is
an extremely serious crime.

263
00:17:56,442 --> 00:17:57,603
You're very fortunate.

264
00:17:57,777 --> 00:18:00,178
All too many kidnap
victims wind up dead.

265
00:18:00,346 --> 00:18:01,678
They never wanted to hurt me.

266
00:18:01,847 --> 00:18:03,782
Look at me, there's
not a scratch on me.

267
00:18:03,950 --> 00:18:06,112
All they wanted to do
was get back at Ben.

268
00:18:07,119 --> 00:18:08,417
Who can blame them?

269
00:18:08,588 --> 00:18:12,081
Sending Barry's
brother to prison like that.

270
00:18:13,960 --> 00:18:16,327
Get him help, preferably
before he testifies.

271
00:18:16,495 --> 00:18:18,225
- I'm not gonna testify.
- What?

272
00:18:18,397 --> 00:18:21,333
I refuse to participate
in this tapestry of justice.

273
00:18:21,500 --> 00:18:23,833
- Travesty of justice, Pop.
- You agree, huh?

274
00:18:24,003 --> 00:18:25,938
Look, Mr. Lewis, if I
don't put you on the stand

275
00:18:26,105 --> 00:18:27,905
the jury's gonna think
that something's wrong.

276
00:18:28,040 --> 00:18:30,202
Something is wrong. These
kids shouldn't be on trial.

277
00:18:30,376 --> 00:18:33,676
You are going to testify,
even if I have to subpoena you.

278
00:18:36,048 --> 00:18:38,711
Look, I believe
we're through here.

279
00:18:38,884 --> 00:18:40,750
I'll see you in court.

280
00:18:42,888 --> 00:18:44,652
No, you won't.

281
00:18:45,591 --> 00:18:46,991
Yes, she will.

282
00:18:47,159 --> 00:18:49,390
And it's not gonna be pretty.

283
00:18:51,731 --> 00:18:53,723
What do you mean,
you marked the bills?

284
00:18:54,400 --> 00:18:56,869
The night before, after I'd
gotten the money together,

285
00:18:57,036 --> 00:19:00,029
while we were all waiting
for the kidnapper to call,

286
00:19:01,173 --> 00:19:02,539
I took a blue pen,

287
00:19:02,708 --> 00:19:05,628
and I made little marks next to
Grant on nearly all the 50s like that one.

288
00:19:05,778 --> 00:19:06,939
Huh.

289
00:19:07,947 --> 00:19:11,782
Dr. Sanborn, heh, do you have
any idea how big a chance you took?

290
00:19:11,951 --> 00:19:14,648
- If the kidnapper had discovered it...
- I know.

291
00:19:14,820 --> 00:19:16,550
I know. Heh, I
can't believe I did it.

292
00:19:16,722 --> 00:19:18,987
And Matthew could
have been killed.

293
00:19:19,158 --> 00:19:20,956
I realize that.

294
00:19:21,227 --> 00:19:23,423
I must have been out of my mind.

295
00:19:25,931 --> 00:19:29,800
Why didn't you say something
when Matthew was released?

296
00:19:30,302 --> 00:19:32,328
Why did you wait till now?

297
00:19:32,738 --> 00:19:35,105
Well, to tell you
the truth, I forgot.

298
00:19:35,274 --> 00:19:37,194
I mean, the money seemed
so hideously unimportant

299
00:19:37,343 --> 00:19:39,710
compared to seeing and
holding Matthew again.

300
00:19:39,879 --> 00:19:44,715
As you know, we've been out of
town, and I just forgot. I'm sorry.

301
00:19:45,051 --> 00:19:46,178
Well, I'll get the word out

302
00:19:46,352 --> 00:19:49,516
and see if any of these 50s
have shown up anywhere.

303
00:19:50,156 --> 00:19:55,459
Depending on his spending habits,
it could lead us to the kidnapper.

304
00:20:02,134 --> 00:20:04,694
You lucked out
once, Dr. Sanborn.

305
00:20:05,237 --> 00:20:06,637
Maybe you will again.

306
00:20:15,715 --> 00:20:19,379
My name is Billy Lewis, and I
live in Willow Springs, Georgia.

307
00:20:19,552 --> 00:20:21,578
Your honor, due to the
reluctance of the witness

308
00:20:21,754 --> 00:20:24,485
to voluntarily cooperate
in providing testimony,

309
00:20:24,657 --> 00:20:27,092
the state asks that he be
treated as a hostile witness.

310
00:20:27,259 --> 00:20:28,887
Proceed.

311
00:20:29,729 --> 00:20:33,894
On February 10th of this year,
you were kidnapped. Is that right?

312
00:20:34,533 --> 00:20:35,557
No.

313
00:20:35,735 --> 00:20:38,364
You were escorted at gunpoint
from the home of Ben Matlock.

314
00:20:38,537 --> 00:20:39,800
Were you not?

315
00:20:39,972 --> 00:20:42,373
Yeah, but I wouldn't
call that being kidnapped.

316
00:20:42,775 --> 00:20:46,644
It was more like, uh, getting an
invitation to a come-as-you-are party

317
00:20:46,812 --> 00:20:48,940
that I didn't feel
like refusing.

318
00:20:49,115 --> 00:20:51,107
Do you recognize those
two people over there?

319
00:20:51,283 --> 00:20:53,946
Yeah. That's Barry
Feldman and Tracey Riggins.

320
00:20:55,221 --> 00:20:56,746
I wanted my son
to be their lawyer,

321
00:20:56,922 --> 00:20:59,619
but he gave me a lot of
gas about conflict of interest.

322
00:20:59,792 --> 00:21:01,192
Are those the two people

323
00:21:01,327 --> 00:21:04,559
who escorted you from Mr. Matlock's
home at gunpoint that day?

324
00:21:05,264 --> 00:21:07,426
Well, I, uh, I went with them.

325
00:21:07,600 --> 00:21:09,796
I'm not sure about
that gunpoint stuff.

326
00:21:09,969 --> 00:21:11,699
They made you
lie down in their van

327
00:21:11,871 --> 00:21:14,670
and then tied your hands together
with duct tape, did they not?

328
00:21:14,840 --> 00:21:17,366
Yeah. But they
didn't tie me very tight.

329
00:21:17,543 --> 00:21:19,739
I could have gotten
away if I'd really wanted to.

330
00:21:28,053 --> 00:21:34,015
They took you to a room and
held you prisoner there for four days.

331
00:21:34,193 --> 00:21:35,991
- Correct?
- No.

332
00:21:36,395 --> 00:21:38,057
They housed me.

333
00:21:38,230 --> 00:21:41,758
They fed me donuts
and played cards with me.

334
00:21:41,934 --> 00:21:46,838
But to tell you the truth, it was really
more like, uh, being on a vacation.

335
00:21:47,006 --> 00:21:49,287
Except you weren't
allowed to leave, were you?

336
00:21:49,408 --> 00:21:50,808
Who said I wanted to?

337
00:21:50,976 --> 00:21:54,777
Mr. Feldman made
several calls to Ben Matlock,

338
00:21:54,947 --> 00:21:57,041
saying that he would kill you

339
00:21:57,216 --> 00:22:00,311
if Mr. Matlock didn't pay him a
large sum of money. Did he not?

340
00:22:01,554 --> 00:22:03,079
Now, that's very interesting

341
00:22:03,255 --> 00:22:06,623
that you should mention money and
Ben Matlock in the same sentence,

342
00:22:06,792 --> 00:22:09,785
- because as far as I'm concerned...
- Just answer the question.

343
00:22:11,030 --> 00:22:13,329
She's got a lot of
nerve calling me hostile.

344
00:22:13,499 --> 00:22:15,661
How long do I have to
put up with this, judge?

345
00:22:19,205 --> 00:22:22,266
I couldn't believe it when he
told me he marked the bills.

346
00:22:23,676 --> 00:22:26,669
We never discussed
doing something like that.

347
00:22:26,846 --> 00:22:28,940
He never even
brought up the subject.

348
00:22:30,049 --> 00:22:32,211
Well, no harm done. The
money's still in the safe.

349
00:22:32,384 --> 00:22:35,252
We'll just have to go out
there, pull all the marked bills.

350
00:22:35,421 --> 00:22:36,912
You can launder them next month.

351
00:22:37,389 --> 00:22:38,413
What's next month?

352
00:22:38,757 --> 00:22:40,117
We have to go to
Europe next month.

353
00:22:40,259 --> 00:22:42,592
- Oh, didn't I tell you?
- Heh.

354
00:22:44,196 --> 00:22:45,391
What for?

355
00:22:45,564 --> 00:22:48,261
- Ooh, I will make up something.
- Hmm.

356
00:22:49,668 --> 00:22:52,297
- Oh, did you get ahold of Becker?
- He's going to meet us there.

357
00:22:52,471 --> 00:22:54,497
- Well, come on, let's go.
- Hang on. Hang on.

358
00:22:57,810 --> 00:23:01,804
Do you see that drunk propped up
against the building across the street?

359
00:23:03,048 --> 00:23:04,072
What about him?

360
00:23:04,683 --> 00:23:07,517
He's wearing $200 boots.

361
00:23:10,723 --> 00:23:13,124
You two take care of the money.

362
00:23:13,292 --> 00:23:16,729
I'm gonna take care of
that bozo once and for all.

363
00:25:20,519 --> 00:25:22,249
Hello, Ed.

364
00:25:22,488 --> 00:25:23,956
These people
wanted to talk with you.

365
00:25:24,123 --> 00:25:28,493
So I got on my car phone and
arranged for a little meeting.

366
00:25:29,795 --> 00:25:31,320
Enjoy.

367
00:25:33,532 --> 00:25:35,398
All right. Come on.

368
00:26:13,172 --> 00:26:14,834
Becker.

369
00:26:24,083 --> 00:26:26,917
- How long have you been here?
- A couple of minutes.

370
00:26:33,258 --> 00:26:34,920
Dr. Sanborn marked
the bills, huh?

371
00:26:35,094 --> 00:26:38,394
Just the 50s. Didn't tell
us about it until today.

372
00:26:42,534 --> 00:26:44,127
Where's Farrell?

373
00:26:44,369 --> 00:26:46,998
Handing Wingate
over to the cops.

374
00:26:47,206 --> 00:26:48,868
Okay.

375
00:26:49,541 --> 00:26:51,635
Pull all the bills that
have a blue ink mark

376
00:26:51,810 --> 00:26:56,339
somewhere in the
vicinity of Grant's face.

377
00:27:07,726 --> 00:27:09,991
I don't see any
ink marks. Do you?

378
00:27:28,981 --> 00:27:31,473
All right. Hands
up. Nobody move.

379
00:27:32,251 --> 00:27:33,742
Check the body.

380
00:27:33,919 --> 00:27:36,354
- He's not breathing.
- Check the pulse.

381
00:27:37,055 --> 00:27:39,217
I had to shoot him.
He went for his gun.

382
00:28:10,122 --> 00:28:13,058
That's him. That's the
guy who kidnapped me.

383
00:28:13,225 --> 00:28:15,319
- Who was he?
- Kurt Becker.

384
00:28:15,494 --> 00:28:18,623
Did five years in Walla Walla for
extortion. Released two years ago.

385
00:28:18,797 --> 00:28:20,959
Looks like he picked
up right where he left off.

386
00:28:21,133 --> 00:28:23,932
Thanks, son. You can go now.

387
00:28:24,670 --> 00:28:26,590
- Thank you, lieutenant.
- Very much appreciated.

388
00:28:26,738 --> 00:28:28,707
Thank you so much.

389
00:28:29,775 --> 00:28:31,004
I just want to thank you again

390
00:28:31,176 --> 00:28:33,475
for going to the FBI and
saying you'd marked those bills.

391
00:28:33,645 --> 00:28:35,765
You know, I'm afraid
I still don't understand

392
00:28:35,914 --> 00:28:38,748
- what that was all about.
- Unfortunately, it's a long story

393
00:28:38,917 --> 00:28:41,614
that didn't quite have the
ending we were hoping for.

394
00:28:41,787 --> 00:28:43,147
The kidnapper's dead.

395
00:28:43,288 --> 00:28:45,450
How could you hope for
a better ending than that?

396
00:28:45,624 --> 00:28:47,252
Well, he died before
we could find out

397
00:28:47,426 --> 00:28:49,952
whether he was working
alone or with somebody.

398
00:28:50,128 --> 00:28:52,029
Got your money back.
At least that's something.

399
00:28:52,197 --> 00:28:53,495
We got Matthew back.

400
00:28:53,665 --> 00:28:56,066
That was all that was
really important. Thanks.

401
00:28:56,235 --> 00:28:57,601
Bye.

402
00:29:06,278 --> 00:29:08,679
- Leanne, did you talk to Wingate yet?
- I'm going there now.

403
00:29:08,847 --> 00:29:10,847
- Good. See you in a minute.
- Where are you going?

404
00:29:15,420 --> 00:29:16,945
Hang on.

405
00:29:19,491 --> 00:29:21,153
Hurry up.

406
00:29:29,101 --> 00:29:31,297
Well, heh, you're just
the person I want to see.

407
00:29:31,470 --> 00:29:33,470
- Not now. Not now.
- Where the hell do you get off

408
00:29:33,639 --> 00:29:35,519
interfering with an FBI
investigation like that?

409
00:29:35,674 --> 00:29:38,974
I've gotta get in there. My daughter
made me drink fruit juice this morning.

410
00:29:39,144 --> 00:29:40,840
- I've gotta get in there.
- Excuse me.

411
00:29:43,048 --> 00:29:44,168
I was following up on a tip.

412
00:29:44,316 --> 00:29:46,156
If you and the cavalry
had arrived any earlier,

413
00:29:46,318 --> 00:29:47,581
you could have blown the thing.

414
00:29:47,753 --> 00:29:50,273
As it is, the perp is dead, and
I'm on leave, all thanks to you.

415
00:29:50,422 --> 00:29:51,446
Now, wait a minute.

416
00:29:51,623 --> 00:29:53,524
You're the one who
pulled the trigger.

417
00:29:53,692 --> 00:29:55,388
What do you mean,
you followed up on a tip?

418
00:29:55,794 --> 00:29:57,023
Just what I said.

419
00:29:57,195 --> 00:29:59,956
Somebody called Agent Farrell and
told her to check out the warehouse.

420
00:30:00,098 --> 00:30:02,618
It was after hours. So she came
to my place and we discussed it.

421
00:30:02,768 --> 00:30:06,034
I went to the warehouse. She helped
the police take your client into custody.

422
00:30:06,204 --> 00:30:08,105
- You got a tip.
- We got a tip.

423
00:30:08,273 --> 00:30:09,605
I got one for you, Mr. Matlock.

424
00:30:10,809 --> 00:30:13,711
Stay off my tail
and out of my face.

425
00:30:14,579 --> 00:30:17,447
Agent Farrell lives pretty
high on the hog. You know that.

426
00:30:21,420 --> 00:30:25,551
Beach cottage in South
Carolina, Porsche in the garage.

427
00:30:25,724 --> 00:30:26,783
What are you getting at?

428
00:30:26,958 --> 00:30:28,984
I said it before.
I'll say it again.

429
00:30:29,161 --> 00:30:32,825
The only way Kurt Becker could
have known to frame Ed Wingate

430
00:30:32,998 --> 00:30:36,127
is if somebody in
the FBI told him to.

431
00:30:36,301 --> 00:30:37,781
I hate to tell you
this, Mr. Matlock,

432
00:30:37,936 --> 00:30:41,668
Agent Farrow was in my office briefing
me on the Sanborn kidnapping case

433
00:30:41,840 --> 00:30:44,002
at the time that Dennis
Spiers was murdered.

434
00:30:44,176 --> 00:30:46,145
So if Ed Wingate
really didn't kill him,

435
00:30:46,311 --> 00:30:47,591
it had to have been Kurt Becker.

436
00:30:48,146 --> 00:30:51,412
Unfortunately he's dead.

437
00:30:52,951 --> 00:30:54,385
Hey.

438
00:30:56,855 --> 00:30:58,551
It was a good plan.

439
00:30:58,724 --> 00:31:00,192
It really was.

440
00:31:00,359 --> 00:31:03,488
I knew there was a
chance I'd get caught.

441
00:31:03,795 --> 00:31:08,733
Just didn't expect Kurt
Becker to get killed. Damn, oh...

442
00:31:08,967 --> 00:31:11,664
- I'm sorry.
- It's okay, heh.

443
00:31:13,939 --> 00:31:15,771
- Sorry I'm late.
- Are you all right?

444
00:31:15,941 --> 00:31:17,910
Oh, I'm much better, thanks.

445
00:31:18,076 --> 00:31:20,272
I had a nice chat
with Agent Pierce.

446
00:31:20,445 --> 00:31:22,565
I hope you asked him how
he wound up in that warehouse

447
00:31:22,714 --> 00:31:24,410
with Kurt Becker and
the ransom money.

448
00:31:24,583 --> 00:31:27,519
Let me guess. An anonymous tip?

449
00:31:27,686 --> 00:31:28,710
Yep.

450
00:31:28,887 --> 00:31:30,253
I'm toast.

451
00:31:30,422 --> 00:31:32,618
Well, maybe it's just as well.

452
00:31:32,791 --> 00:31:38,628
I've obviously lost my edge, my ability
to outthink and outdo my opponents.

453
00:31:38,797 --> 00:31:42,199
My professional
efficiency is defunct.

454
00:31:42,367 --> 00:31:44,768
Oh, shut up.

455
00:31:53,178 --> 00:31:57,582
We, the jury, find the defendants,
Barry Feldman and Tracey Riggins,

456
00:31:57,749 --> 00:31:59,217
guilty of kidnapping.

457
00:32:04,656 --> 00:32:06,656
Thank you, ladies and
gentlemen of the jury,

458
00:32:06,825 --> 00:32:08,088
for a job well done.

459
00:32:08,460 --> 00:32:10,793
You are excused from
further duty in this case

460
00:32:10,962 --> 00:32:13,193
and free to leave
this courtroom.

461
00:32:14,566 --> 00:32:15,590
Court is adjourned.

462
00:32:16,368 --> 00:32:17,996
- Dad, Dad, Dad.
- I'm so sorry.

463
00:32:18,170 --> 00:32:20,850
Hey, take it easy. It's all right. I
mean, we did it. We should pay.

464
00:32:21,006 --> 00:32:23,271
- Yeah, but you're gonna go to prison.
- That's okay.

465
00:32:23,442 --> 00:32:25,343
It'll give Tracey a chance
to lose some weight,

466
00:32:25,510 --> 00:32:27,001
and I'll get to
visit my brother.

467
00:32:27,179 --> 00:32:28,909
It's all my fault.

468
00:32:29,080 --> 00:32:31,549
Billy, we kidnapped
you, remember?

469
00:32:31,716 --> 00:32:33,516
Yeah, but you didn't
have anything against me.

470
00:32:33,652 --> 00:32:35,621
You just wanted
to get back at Ben.

471
00:32:35,787 --> 00:32:37,187
Yeah. Still do.

472
00:32:37,355 --> 00:32:39,756
- Yeah. That cheapskate.
- What a jackass.

473
00:32:39,925 --> 00:32:42,986
I knew I liked you guys.

474
00:33:11,590 --> 00:33:13,821
What a pleasant surprise.

475
00:33:15,494 --> 00:33:17,053
Come on in.

476
00:33:28,306 --> 00:33:30,207
Why so quiet?

477
00:33:31,276 --> 00:33:32,869
Sit down.

478
00:33:42,287 --> 00:33:43,846
Matlock knows about this place.

479
00:33:45,991 --> 00:33:47,983
Knows about the Porsche.

480
00:33:48,727 --> 00:33:51,356
He's been asking
a lot of questions.

481
00:33:52,597 --> 00:33:54,293
So? Heh.

482
00:33:54,633 --> 00:33:57,000
As long as he can't prove
anything, who cares?

483
00:33:58,069 --> 00:34:01,267
Thing is, the pressure
is getting to you.

484
00:34:01,439 --> 00:34:03,305
You're getting
worried, depressed.

485
00:34:05,010 --> 00:34:06,638
Heh, I am?

486
00:34:10,215 --> 00:34:11,895
You're afraid everyone
is going to find out

487
00:34:12,050 --> 00:34:14,246
you've been getting
rich kidnapping children.

488
00:34:14,886 --> 00:34:17,651
You can't face the humiliation.

489
00:34:18,456 --> 00:34:20,049
So,

490
00:34:24,262 --> 00:34:25,560
you kill yourself.

491
00:34:25,730 --> 00:34:27,096
Come on.

492
00:34:34,039 --> 00:34:35,598
Freeze.

493
00:34:37,776 --> 00:34:39,642
Give it up, Pierce.

494
00:34:46,885 --> 00:34:50,720
I know you told me to stay off your
tail, but I just couldn't help myself.

495
00:34:57,395 --> 00:35:00,229
We pulled off four kidnappings,

496
00:35:00,532 --> 00:35:03,434
not only the Sanborn case.

497
00:35:04,703 --> 00:35:09,004
Kurt Becker would make the
snatch and see to it nobody got hurt

498
00:35:09,174 --> 00:35:12,633
while Alan and I would make
sure he made a clean getaway.

499
00:35:13,612 --> 00:35:15,274
We'd meet up later
and split the ransom.

500
00:35:15,447 --> 00:35:16,881
How much money did you make?

501
00:35:17,048 --> 00:35:20,314
Each of us took in close to 400
thousand dollars in two years.

502
00:35:20,485 --> 00:35:22,954
Dennis Spiers,
how did he fit in?

503
00:35:23,121 --> 00:35:25,522
Kurt Becker saw him
snooping around his place.

504
00:35:27,325 --> 00:35:30,261
Figured he was getting
too close for comfort.

505
00:35:31,162 --> 00:35:32,926
He called me.

506
00:35:33,131 --> 00:35:35,430
I called Alan Pierce.

507
00:35:36,167 --> 00:35:37,999
Next thing I knew,
Dennis was dead.

508
00:35:38,570 --> 00:35:39,970
Who killed him?

509
00:35:41,172 --> 00:35:43,903
- It wasn't me. MAN:
Where were you that night?

510
00:35:45,276 --> 00:35:49,077
Heh, as a matter of fact, I
was out in Glendale Hills,

511
00:35:49,247 --> 00:35:51,079
casing the home of
a prospective victim.

512
00:35:51,249 --> 00:35:52,889
You can't prove where
you were that night.

513
00:35:53,018 --> 00:35:56,113
Uh, actually, I can.

514
00:35:56,921 --> 00:35:59,618
A private security guard pulled
up next to us in his patrol car

515
00:35:59,791 --> 00:36:01,350
and asked us what we were doing.

516
00:36:01,526 --> 00:36:03,757
I flashed my shield
and told him to get lost.

517
00:36:04,796 --> 00:36:06,389
I'm sure he'll remember.

518
00:36:07,065 --> 00:36:08,397
You were with somebody.

519
00:36:08,867 --> 00:36:10,358
Kurt Becker.

520
00:36:19,344 --> 00:36:21,939
Well, it wasn't her, and
it wasn't Kurt Becker.

521
00:36:22,113 --> 00:36:24,605
- It could have been Ed Wingate.
- Oh, come on.

522
00:36:24,783 --> 00:36:27,218
This kidnapping ring
stuff's all very interesting,

523
00:36:27,385 --> 00:36:29,980
but when it comes to the
murder of Dennis Spiers,

524
00:36:30,155 --> 00:36:31,714
the facts still point
to Ed Wingate,

525
00:36:31,890 --> 00:36:35,383
and since you seem to be going up
against a 20-year veteran of the FBI,

526
00:36:35,560 --> 00:36:37,028
chances are they always will.

527
00:36:37,195 --> 00:36:39,630
- Hmm.
- I'm sorry.

528
00:36:49,474 --> 00:36:51,670
Dennis Spiers is supposed
to be a good agent.

529
00:36:51,843 --> 00:36:53,539
Maybe he left us a clue.

530
00:36:53,712 --> 00:36:55,305
Tape recorded
message would be nice.

531
00:36:55,480 --> 00:36:56,641
Huh.

532
00:36:58,049 --> 00:36:59,210
Well, let's see.

533
00:36:59,384 --> 00:37:02,377
Now, he is, uh... He
was on the telephone.

534
00:37:03,455 --> 00:37:05,720
Hello, uh-huh.

535
00:37:05,890 --> 00:37:07,916
Yeah. Yeah. Conversation.

536
00:37:08,560 --> 00:37:10,586
And then he hung it up.

537
00:37:11,229 --> 00:37:13,391
And then he turned around
and there was the killer.

538
00:37:13,565 --> 00:37:14,931
And he got shot, ooh.

539
00:37:15,100 --> 00:37:16,432
But he didn't die right away.

540
00:37:16,601 --> 00:37:18,331
He put up a big fight.

541
00:37:18,503 --> 00:37:21,697
Fighting him, hitting him...

542
00:37:21,873 --> 00:37:23,000
And he got hit again.

543
00:37:25,110 --> 00:37:27,978
Then he fell down, unh. Dead.

544
00:37:29,347 --> 00:37:30,542
And then the...

545
00:37:30,715 --> 00:37:33,651
Oh, takes stuff out of his
pockets and some other stuff

546
00:37:33,818 --> 00:37:36,982
to make it look like
it was a robbery.

547
00:37:41,726 --> 00:37:44,958
- Oh, Dad, look at this.
- Heh, what?

548
00:37:45,230 --> 00:37:46,823
Get up here and look at this.

549
00:37:47,966 --> 00:37:49,559
Are you gonna give me a hand?

550
00:37:50,502 --> 00:37:53,301
You know, you are too old
to be falling down like this.

551
00:37:53,471 --> 00:37:57,431
I'm not too old to be
falling down like this.

552
00:37:57,609 --> 00:37:59,305
Hurt myself.

553
00:38:02,080 --> 00:38:03,571
Come here. Come here. Come here.

554
00:38:03,748 --> 00:38:05,273
Look. Look at this.

555
00:38:07,819 --> 00:38:11,017
- Now, look at this.
- Oh!

556
00:38:21,099 --> 00:38:24,797
How long have you been
with the FBI, Agent Pierce?

557
00:38:24,969 --> 00:38:27,200
- Going on 23 years.
- Uh-huh.

558
00:38:27,472 --> 00:38:29,100
And, uh, what's
your current salary?

559
00:38:29,274 --> 00:38:30,469
Sixty-five thousand dollars.

560
00:38:30,642 --> 00:38:31,769
You married?

561
00:38:31,943 --> 00:38:35,311
Yes, but, uh, my wife and I have
been separated for almost a year now.

562
00:38:35,480 --> 00:38:37,915
You have an account in
a bank in the Bahamas,

563
00:38:38,082 --> 00:38:40,176
don't you? PIERCE: Yes.

564
00:38:40,351 --> 00:38:42,911
And only you have
access to that account?

565
00:38:43,488 --> 00:38:44,512
Yes.

566
00:38:44,689 --> 00:38:47,625
So all the money that's in it is
there because you put it there.

567
00:38:48,159 --> 00:38:49,559
Yes.

568
00:38:50,061 --> 00:38:54,499
Uh, here are some records from
that bank that we subpoenaed.

569
00:38:54,666 --> 00:38:59,161
It's got all your
deposits and balances.

570
00:38:59,337 --> 00:39:03,900
Would you just read
your current balance?

571
00:39:04,742 --> 00:39:09,112
413,690 dollars.

572
00:39:09,280 --> 00:39:12,250
Get out of town.

573
00:39:12,483 --> 00:39:17,683
How can a man that makes
sixty-five thousand dollars a year

574
00:39:17,856 --> 00:39:19,620
get his hands on
that kind of money?

575
00:39:20,959 --> 00:39:22,239
I guess I just know how to save.

576
00:39:22,393 --> 00:39:23,417
Heh, hmm.

577
00:39:24,062 --> 00:39:27,590
You were recently arrested
and charged with kidnapping,

578
00:39:27,765 --> 00:39:29,324
- weren't you?
- Yes.

579
00:39:29,500 --> 00:39:32,493
And an agent under your
supervision named Brenda Farrell

580
00:39:32,670 --> 00:39:35,401
was arrested and
charged for kidnapping, too.

581
00:39:35,840 --> 00:39:37,775
- Yes.
- Only, she confessed.

582
00:39:38,276 --> 00:39:44,182
She said that you and a man named
Kurt Becker and she were partners.

583
00:39:44,349 --> 00:39:45,544
That doesn't make it true.

584
00:39:45,717 --> 00:39:47,652
But you did kill Kurt
Becker, didn't you?

585
00:39:47,819 --> 00:39:49,845
In the line of duty, yes.

586
00:39:50,021 --> 00:39:54,391
And you were arrested for trying
to kill agent Farrell, weren't you?

587
00:39:55,360 --> 00:39:59,627
Again, just because agent Farrell
says something does not mean it's true.

588
00:39:59,797 --> 00:40:02,289
And when she told you that, uh,

589
00:40:02,467 --> 00:40:05,494
Dennis Spiers was
closing in on Kurt Becker,

590
00:40:05,670 --> 00:40:07,550
you killed him, too, so
that he would never know

591
00:40:07,705 --> 00:40:12,507
that you and Kurt Becker and
agent Farrell were in cahoots.

592
00:40:12,677 --> 00:40:14,043
No. I did not.

593
00:40:14,212 --> 00:40:15,680
Hmm.

594
00:40:16,681 --> 00:40:22,245
When, uh, was the last time you
were in, uh, Agent Spiers' house?

595
00:40:22,420 --> 00:40:23,444
The night he died.

596
00:40:23,621 --> 00:40:25,381
The police called me
when the body was found.

597
00:40:25,523 --> 00:40:28,459
- So you were there the night he died.
- That's correct.

598
00:40:28,626 --> 00:40:34,930
The reason I ask
is, uh, this a blow-up

599
00:40:35,099 --> 00:40:38,968
uh, of one of the police
photos of the, uh, crime scene.

600
00:40:39,137 --> 00:40:42,869
You see that stack of mail
there, with that plastic card on it,

601
00:40:43,041 --> 00:40:45,567
with the magnetic,
uh, line there?

602
00:40:45,743 --> 00:40:48,838
Yes, uh, that's Dennis' pass
key to the garage at headquarters.

603
00:40:49,013 --> 00:40:50,276
Yeah.

604
00:40:50,448 --> 00:40:51,780
Well, that's not quite true.

605
00:40:51,950 --> 00:40:54,181
This is a blow-up
of that blow-up.

606
00:40:54,352 --> 00:40:59,814
See. If you look carefully, you
can read the name on this pass card.

607
00:41:00,591 --> 00:41:01,786
Could you read it aloud?

608
00:41:07,265 --> 00:41:08,665
- Alan Pierce.
- Alan Pierce.

609
00:41:08,833 --> 00:41:10,802
That's your pass key.

610
00:41:10,969 --> 00:41:15,373
What's your pass key doing
at the scene of the crime?

611
00:41:16,507 --> 00:41:18,271
I know what
happened. They, uh...

612
00:41:18,876 --> 00:41:20,708
They issued that pass
key the day before,

613
00:41:20,878 --> 00:41:23,677
only Dennis was so busy on the
Sanborn case he never got his.

614
00:41:23,848 --> 00:41:25,680
So I swung by his
house to drop it off.

615
00:41:27,018 --> 00:41:29,852
I must have given him the one
with my name on it by mistake.

616
00:41:30,021 --> 00:41:32,217
Ah, that's what
happened all right.

617
00:41:32,390 --> 00:41:35,554
Dennis Spiers, it's a simple mix up.
I just never noticed it until just now.

618
00:41:35,727 --> 00:41:41,826
You're saying, this all occurred
the night before the murder.

619
00:41:42,000 --> 00:41:44,697
That's right. I, uh, told my
secretary where I was going and why.

620
00:41:44,869 --> 00:41:47,202
- You can ask her.
- Yeah. Well, okay.

621
00:41:47,372 --> 00:41:51,639
Well, look at the date
on this postmark here.

622
00:41:52,377 --> 00:41:54,437
- It says... Get your glasses.
- Hmm.

623
00:41:54,612 --> 00:41:58,743
It says February 11th, see that?

624
00:41:59,283 --> 00:42:00,649
He had just
brought this mail in.

625
00:42:00,818 --> 00:42:03,151
He hadn't even gotten
around to opening it up.

626
00:42:03,321 --> 00:42:08,191
Yet here's your pass key
right smack dab on top of it.

627
00:42:09,427 --> 00:42:10,486
So what?

628
00:42:10,661 --> 00:42:13,290
He probably used it
all day, like I used his,

629
00:42:13,464 --> 00:42:15,456
got home after work,
took it out of his pocket,

630
00:42:15,633 --> 00:42:18,193
dropped it on his desk,
where it landed on his mail.

631
00:42:18,369 --> 00:42:19,428
You're right.

632
00:42:19,604 --> 00:42:21,436
He did, he used his all day.

633
00:42:21,606 --> 00:42:28,410
He was in and out of that office a lot
working on the Sanborn kidnapping.

634
00:42:29,047 --> 00:42:32,142
I had, uh, Lieutenant Bronson

635
00:42:32,316 --> 00:42:36,219
remove the pass key in question

636
00:42:36,621 --> 00:42:38,647
from, uh, Mr. Spiers' house,

637
00:42:38,823 --> 00:42:43,818
and he had the lab
ID the fingerprints on it.

638
00:42:43,995 --> 00:42:46,760
Your fingerprints
are all over it.

639
00:42:46,931 --> 00:42:50,868
You know how many of
Dennis Spiers' prints are on it?

640
00:42:51,035 --> 00:42:52,435
None.

641
00:42:53,237 --> 00:42:54,364
None. Now, how...?

642
00:42:54,539 --> 00:42:58,533
How could he have
used this pass key all day

643
00:42:58,709 --> 00:43:01,110
without leaving any
fingerprints on it?

644
00:43:01,913 --> 00:43:05,406
You're right. He
couldn't. He couldn't.

645
00:43:05,583 --> 00:43:10,078
The only way this card could have
wound up on top of this unopened mail

646
00:43:10,254 --> 00:43:13,554
was there was
some kind of mix-up.

647
00:43:14,592 --> 00:43:17,892
It fell out of your pocket
during the struggle, didn't it?

648
00:43:18,062 --> 00:43:19,325
Landed on that mail.

649
00:43:20,731 --> 00:43:24,065
And after it was over,
after you had killed him,

650
00:43:24,235 --> 00:43:28,036
you picked up his card and left.

651
00:43:29,640 --> 00:43:33,270
And when you found
out about the fight

652
00:43:34,045 --> 00:43:37,243
that my client, Ed
Wingate, had had with him,

653
00:43:37,415 --> 00:43:40,715
you told Kurt
Becker to frame him

654
00:43:40,885 --> 00:43:43,514
by planting the murder
weapon at his house.

655
00:43:47,291 --> 00:43:51,422
If you've got some other explanation,
I'm sure the jury would like to hear it.

656
00:43:57,301 --> 00:43:58,894
That's what I thought.

657
00:44:19,457 --> 00:44:21,050
Sure you don't
want a cup of coffee?

658
00:44:21,225 --> 00:44:22,825
No, thanks. I'll grab
some at the office.

659
00:44:22,994 --> 00:44:25,275
You're really looking forward
to getting back, aren't you?

660
00:44:25,429 --> 00:44:27,227
Fighting crime is
my life, Leanne.

661
00:44:27,798 --> 00:44:29,824
In fact, if either of you
ever has any trouble

662
00:44:30,001 --> 00:44:33,870
that involves the smuggling of
drugs or illegal weapons, mail fraud,

663
00:44:34,038 --> 00:44:36,667
bank robbery, racketeering,
anything like that,

664
00:44:36,841 --> 00:44:41,370
rest assured, you
have a friend in the FBI.

665
00:44:41,546 --> 00:44:43,344
That's very reassuring.

666
00:44:43,514 --> 00:44:47,007
Ben, yoo-hoo.

667
00:44:48,819 --> 00:44:50,253
In the kitchen, Billy.

668
00:44:50,421 --> 00:44:52,253
I guess he's not
mad at you anymore.

669
00:44:52,423 --> 00:44:54,551
Hmm, it didn't last.

670
00:44:55,326 --> 00:44:56,487
Cliff, you remember Ed.

671
00:44:56,661 --> 00:45:00,325
Billy Lewis, Ed
Wingate, FBI agent.

672
00:45:03,034 --> 00:45:04,127
Never mind.

673
00:45:04,302 --> 00:45:07,067
- Would you like some coffee?
- Mm, mm.

674
00:45:11,342 --> 00:45:15,370
Ben, I owe you an apology.

675
00:45:16,614 --> 00:45:19,243
In fact, I very well
may owe you my life.

676
00:45:20,618 --> 00:45:22,917
I just want you to
know I'm grateful.

677
00:45:24,689 --> 00:45:26,180
Really?

678
00:45:28,559 --> 00:45:29,583
Come here.

679
00:45:31,596 --> 00:45:33,895
- What?
- I want to give you a hug.

680
00:45:34,398 --> 00:45:36,492
Guess who's been
seeing a therapist.

681
00:45:36,667 --> 00:45:40,434
Yeah, he's been helping me deal
with the trauma of being kidnapped.

682
00:45:40,605 --> 00:45:42,725
In fact, at first, I was gonna
make you pay his bills.

683
00:45:43,441 --> 00:45:44,465
What?

684
00:45:44,642 --> 00:45:48,704
But then I realized that that kind of
hostility doesn't get anybody anywhere.

685
00:45:50,348 --> 00:45:53,716
It's time to become
friends, Ben.

686
00:45:53,884 --> 00:45:58,151
It's time to make,
heh, real contact.

687
00:45:59,323 --> 00:46:01,792
- Come here.
- No.

688
00:46:01,959 --> 00:46:04,428
- It'll make us both feel better.
- No.

689
00:46:04,595 --> 00:46:06,461
- Yes, it will. Come on.
- No.

690
00:46:06,631 --> 00:46:07,792
- Yeah.
- No.

691
00:46:07,965 --> 00:46:09,194
Dad.

692
00:46:14,605 --> 00:46:16,096
- Come on.
- Let's go.

693
00:46:16,274 --> 00:46:17,765
It's time.

694
00:46:33,658 --> 00:46:35,752
- You're suffocating me.
- Good.

695
00:46:36,927 --> 00:46:39,954
- Moron.
- Jackass.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
